<Layout title="Research">
  <h1>Research</h1>
  <p>
    Rotator cuff tears are among the most common shoulder injuries, and
    surgery can reattach the tendon, but the muscle often doesn't come
    back. It wastes away, fills with fat and scar tissue, and those changes
    can persist long after repair. My research asks whether a material
    injected into the muscle itself can change that healing environment.
  </p>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Kevin Healy Lab, UC Berkeley</h3>
    <p class="pub-meta">PhD Candidate &middot; January 2022 &ndash; August 2026</p>

    <p style="font-weight: 700; color: var(--pink-700); margin-bottom: 0.2rem;">
      Viscoelastic Hyaluronic Acid Hydrogels for Muscle Regeneration
    </p>
    <p>
      I engineer tunable, semi-synthetic hyaluronic acid (HyA) hydrogels
      whose viscoelastic mechanics are designed to support muscle
      regeneration and vascularization after injuries like delayed rotator
      cuff repair and volumetric muscle loss. Much of my work is on the
      materials side, where I tune and characterize the gels' mechanics and
      injectability and develop drug-loaded versions carrying formoterol.
    </p>
    <p>
      To understand <em>why</em> the gels work, I study how they steer
      fibro-adipogenic progenitors (FAPs), the muscle-resident cells that
      drive fat buildup after injury, toward a brown-fat-like phenotype in
      3D culture. I also performed all of the single-cell RNA-seq analysis of
      patient-derived FAPs and used what we learned to redesign the
      hydrogels. In collaboration with orthopedic surgeons, I prepared
      hydrogels for and analyzed data from in vivo injury studies.
    </p>
    <p>
      This work has been published in <em>Advanced Healthcare Materials</em>
      and presented at BMES, the Society For Biomaterials, the Orthopaedic Research Society, and
      Biological Scaffolds for Regenerative Medicine.
    </p>
    <div class="home-cta">
      <a class="btn secondary" href="https://biomaterials.berkeley.edu/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        Visit the Healy Lab
      </a>
      <a class="btn secondary" href="/publications/">See Publications</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>MuscleMatrix</h3>
    <p class="pub-meta">Scientific Consultant &middot; August 2026 &ndash; Present</p>

    <p style="font-weight: 700; color: var(--pink-700); margin-bottom: 0.2rem;">
      From the Bench Toward the Clinic
    </p>
    <p>
      MuscleMatrix is an early-stage startup built on the hydrogel
      technology I co-invented during my PhD, developing an injectable,
      cell-free hyaluronic acid scaffold to support muscle repair after
      rotator cuff injury. As a scientific consultant, I'm helping build the
      company from the ground up, which includes setting up a lab from scratch, regulatory
      and safety compliance, sourcing manufacturers and suppliers, FDA
      regulatory strategy, and grant writing. I also continue formulation
      work on injectability and mechanics, and help train the company's newest
      scientist.
    </p>
    <a class="btn secondary" href="https://ipira.berkeley.edu/musclematrix" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
      Learn More About MuscleMatrix
    </a>
  </div>

  <h2>Earlier research</h2>
  <p>
    Before Berkeley, I worked across materials science, cancer biology, and
    drug discovery, and each stop shaped how I approach research today.
  </p>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Randall Erb Lab (DAPS), Northeastern University</h3>
    <p class="pub-meta">Biomaterials Undergraduate Researcher &middot; September 2016 &ndash; July 2021</p>
    <img
      src="/daps-hydrogel.jpg"
      alt="A dual-network hydrogel sample held with forceps in a gloved hand"
      class="card-img"
    />
    <p>
      In the Directed Assembly of Particles and Suspensions lab, I developed
      dual-network hydrogels reinforced with magnetically aligned
      hydroxyapatite fibers for bone grafts, used COMSOL simulations to
      explore how fiber rotation might toughen them (work that won the RISE
      Excellence in Research Award), and fabricated freeze-cast nerve
      repair conduits with collaborators at Dartmouth College and Harvard
      Medical School. I also wrote the proposal that funded my research
      through a PEAK Summit Award.
    </p>
    <a class="btn secondary" href="https://www.dapslab.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
      Visit the DAPS Lab
    </a>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Marc Hansen Lab, UConn Health</h3>
    <p class="pub-meta">Summer Research Fellow &middot; Summers 2017 &amp; 2018</p>
    <p>
      Over two summers, I piloted synthetic-lethal experiments to find
      druggable vulnerabilities in osteosarcoma, and used laser capture
      microdissection to isolate bone cell populations and study Paget's
      disease of bone.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Vertex Pharmaceuticals</h3>
    <p class="pub-meta">Exploratory Research Co-op &middot; January 2020 &ndash; June 2020</p>
    <p>
      I developed cellular assays to probe potentially druggable pathways
      for sickle cell disease and built stable cell lines for the
      exploratory research team.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Editas Medicine</h3>
    <p class="pub-meta">Screening Group Co-op &middot; January 2018 &ndash; June 2018</p>
    <p>
      I screened CRISPR guide RNAs for the company's pipeline diseases and
      programmed high-throughput screening assays on automated liquid
      handlers.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
